# DURABLE REPRO — B2: the by-value `&`-projection write-through bug with a
# BARE-PARAM root. This is the row a naive fix breaks.
#
# `via_bare` takes `Holder h` BARE, which under CoW-default-borrow is a BORROW.
# The write `bump_int(&h.i)` therefore MATERIALIZES a private copy at the
# closest immutable context: the callee observes its own write, and the CALLER'S
# object is UNCHANGED.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): prints
#     10 / 10
# The caller's 10 is right by accident — the write is lost everywhere, including
# inside the callee, which is the same defect as
# `sound_amp_byvalue_place_writethrough.gg`.
#
# ORACLE: ggdef prints 11 / 10 — the materialized copy is written, the caller is
# untouched.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): 11 / 10.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE ROW MOST LIKELY TO REGRESS SILENTLY. A fix that makes the
# `&`-projection write THROUGH to the root looks correct on every other fixture
# in the batch and is WRONG here — it would print 11 / 11, turning a borrow into
# a mutable alias and breaking CoW's central guarantee. Whoever fixes the class
# must land this fixture green at 11 / 10, not merely make the caller's 10 stay
# 10 (which it already does today, for the wrong reason).
#
# The seed is computed, not a literal.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High): "By-value-field `&`-arg
# write-through is SILENTLY LOST" (bare-param-root row).
struct Holder:
    int i

int seed():
    return 10

void bump_int(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void via_bare(Holder h):
    bump_int(&h.i)
    print(h.i)

void main():
    Holder h = Holder(seed())
    via_bare(h)
    print(h.i)
